# ── worker-standalone.env.example — copy to `worker-standalone.env` ─────────
#
#   cp worker-standalone.env.example worker-standalone.env
#   chmod 600 worker-standalone.env
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.worker.yml up -d --build
#
# `## 405.` added this file. `docker-compose.worker.yml` has always declared
# `env_file: - worker-standalone.env`, and that name is gitignored, so a fresh
# clone had no such file and no template for one: the compose command in the
# service README aborted before building anything, on Compose's own words about
# a missing env file, and nothing in the repository said what belonged in it.
# `.env.example` existed for serve.py's `.env`; the two worker env files had no
# equivalent.
#
# NOTHING IN HERE IS A SECRET. Every value is a placeholder. Never commit the
# filled-in `worker-standalone.env` — it is gitignored for that reason.
#
# HUMAN-ONLY GATE: WORKER_PRINCIPAL/WORKER_SECRET are issued by registering a
# worker in ai.cafreso.com's settings, and BRAVE_API_KEY comes from Brave's own
# dashboard. Both are accounts a person has to create; no local step produces
# them, and the registration is a mainnet action. A tester cannot self-serve
# this path — see docs/BETA_READINESS.md.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SEARCH_WORKER=1
# Principal registered via ai.cafreso.com → settings. Use a FRESH, second
# identity here rather than reusing docker-compose.local.yml's, so that while
# both instances run side by side you can tell which one claimed which job.
WORKER_PRINCIPAL=
# The 64-hex secret shown ONCE at registration. If you lost it, re-register.
WORKER_SECRET=
# https://brave.com/search/api/ — the free tier is enough to validate.
BRAVE_API_KEY=

# ── Optional: which brain answers the jobs ──────────────────────────────────
# Local env always wins over the operator's published default. See worker.py's
# module docstring for the full precedence rules.
# WORKER_MODEL=
# WORKER_BACKEND_URL=
# WORKER_BACKEND_KEY=

# ── Optional: crons ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONLY ONE instance across your whole deployment may have these on at a time.
# docker-compose.worker.yml pins all three to "0" in its `environment:` block,
# which OVERRIDES anything you set here — flip them there, at the cutover step.
# GAP_CRON=0
# NEWS_CRON=0
# TOPICS_CRON=0

# ── Optional: status endpoint ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# STATUS_PORT=8788
# STATUS_ALLOW_ORIGIN=
